canonical_claim.statement:
Printed. PDF p. 23, Appendix D.3, first paragraph: 'Suppose the principal estimates $\hat\mu_0(x)$ from the untreated data from the agent's treatment population, i.e. those individuals for whom $T_i^\pi=0$. As discussed in Sections 5 and A, under full information symmetry, the mean conditional untreated potential outcome is identifiable as long as the agent's treatment rule class $\Pi$ maintains positivity, $\pi(x)>0$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$.' PDF p. 23: 'Theorem 5. Suppose for all $\pi\in\Pi$, $\pi(x,u)>0$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$, $u\in\mathcal U$. If the principal applies the reward function $w_{TT}$ using an estimate $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)$, then under Assumption 4, the regret is upper bounded as $R(\pi^w)\leq2\gamma_{max}$.' PDF p. 24 (proof of Theorem 5): 'Since for all $\pi\in\Pi$, $\pi(x,u)>0$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$, $u\in\mathcal U$, we can apply Lemma 3 below, which says that $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)\in[\min_u\mu_0(x,u),\max_u\mu_0(x,u)]$. Therefore, for all $x,u$, $\Delta^\Pi(x,u)\leq\Delta(x,u)$.' PDF p. 24: 'Lemma 3. Suppose that for all $\pi\in\Pi$, $P_{U|X=x,T^\pi=0}$ is a well defined probability distribution. Let $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)=E[Y|X=x,T^\pi=0]$. Then for all $\pi$, $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)\in[\min_u\mu_0(x,u),\max_u\mu_0(x,u)]$.'

Corrected statement. Setting (S8) with (S9), an arbitrary feasible set $\Pi$ of functions $\pi:\mathcal X\times\mathcal U\to[0,1]$, and the population quantity $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)=E[Y_i|X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0]$. Write $q_\pi(x)=P(T^\pi=0\mid X=x)=E[1-\pi(x,U)\mid X=x]$. Theorem 5 (corrected): suppose that for every $\pi\in\Pi$, $q_\pi(x)>0$ for $P_X$-almost every $x$. If the principal applies the reward function $w_{\mathrm{TT}}$ using the estimate $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)$, then under Assumption 4 the regret is upper bounded as $R(\pi^w)\leq2\gamma_{\mathrm{max}}$. In detail, for every $\pi\in\Pi$:
(i) (Lemma 3) $P_{U|X=x,T^\pi=0}(du)=\frac{1-\pi(x,u)}{q_\pi(x)}P_{U|X=x}(du)$ is a probability distribution, $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)=\int\mu_0(x,u)\,dP_{U|X=x,T^\pi=0}(u)$, and $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)\in[\min_u\mu_0(x,u),\max_u\mu_0(x,u)]$;
(ii) $|\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)-\mu_0(x,u)|\leq\Delta(x,u)$ for $P_X$-almost every $x$ and every $u$;
(iii) with $S(\pi)=\frac1nE[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=E[\pi(X,U)(\mu_1(X,U)-\hat\mu_0^\pi(X))]$: $|S(\pi)-V(\pi)|\leq E[|\hat\mu_0^\pi(X)-\mu_0(X,U)|]\leq E[\Delta(X,U)]\leq\gamma_{\mathrm{max}}$;
(iv) for $\pi^w\in\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}S(\pi)$ and $\pi^*\in\arg\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)$: $R(\pi^w)=V(\pi^*)-V(\pi^w)\leq2\gamma_{\mathrm{max}}$.
The pointwise condition $\pi(x,u)<1$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$, $u\in\mathcal U$ implies $q_\pi(x)>0$ and is stronger than needed. First paragraph of D.3 (corrected): under full information symmetry ($\pi$ depends on $x$ only), $\mu_0(x)=E[Y_i|X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0]$ at every $x$ with $\pi(x)<1$, so the condition is 'positivity, $\pi(x)<1$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$'.

Printed. PDF p. 23: 'Assumption 4 is stronger than Assumption 2 in the sense that Assumption 2 is not sufficient to bound the regret when the principal estimates the mean conditional untreated potential outcome from the agent's data using $\hat\mu_0^\pi(X)$. Furthermore, the bound in Assumption 4 implies that Assumption 2 is satisfied with $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}\leq\gamma_{\mathrm{max}}$. Intuitively, Assumption 4 is not sufficient to bound the regret when the principal uses $\hat\mu_0^\pi(X)$ because the agent can choose $\pi$ to allocate treatment to single values of $\tilde u\in\mathcal U$, such that $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)$ ends up matching a single value $\mu_0(x,\tilde u)$.'

Corrected statement. 'Intuitively, Assumption 2 is not sufficient to bound the regret when the principal uses $\hat\mu_0^\pi(X)$, because the agent can leave only a selected positive-mass type $\tilde u$ untreated, so that $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)=\mu_0(x,\tilde u)$.' Formal content. Fix $x$ and let $U$ given $X=x$ take finitely many values $u$ with probabilities $p_u>0$; let $\pi(x,\cdot)$ satisfy $\sum_up_u(1-\pi(x,u))>0$. Then:
(a) $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)=E[Y|X=x,T^\pi=0]=\dfrac{\sum_u\mu_0(x,u)\,p_u(1-\pi(x,u))}{\sum_up_u(1-\pi(x,u))}$;
(b) if $\pi(x,\tilde u)=0$ and $\pi(x,u)=1$ for every $u\neq\tilde u$ (only the type $\tilde u$ is left untreated), then $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)=\mu_0(x,\tilde u)$;
(c) if instead $\pi(x,\tilde u)=1$ and $\pi(x,u)=0$ for every $u\neq\tilde u$ (treatment is allocated to the single value $\tilde u$, as printed), and there is at least one other type, then $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)$ is the $p$-weighted mean of $\mu_0(x,u)$ over $u\neq\tilde u$. Example: three equally likely types with $\mu_0(x,\cdot)=0,1,3$; treating only the first type gives $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)=2\neq0$, and leaving only the first type untreated gives $\hat\mu_0^\pi(x)=0$.
(d) The companion statement of the same paragraph holds as printed: $E[|\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i,U_i)|]\leq E[\Delta(X,U)]$, so Assumption 4 with the constant $\gamma_{\mathrm{max}}$ implies Assumption 2 with $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}=\gamma_{\mathrm{max}}$.

Printed. PDF p. 17: 'A second failure mode would be if $\alpha=-1$, and $\beta>1$. Then the regret is given by $R(\pi^w)=(1-p)-p(\beta-1)=1-p\beta$. This is also unbounded for unbounded $\beta$.'

Corrected statement. In the family of the proof ($\mathcal X=\{0,1\}$, $P(X_i=1)=p\in(0,1)$, $\mu_1(0)=0$, $\mu_1(1)=1$, $\mu_0(0)=\alpha=-1$, $\mu_0(1)=\beta>1$, $\Pi$ the set of all functions $\pi:\mathcal X\to[0,1]$, written $\pi=(a,b)$), with $\pi^w=(0,1)$ as the proof states:
(i) $\tau(0)=1$, $\tau(1)=1-\beta<0$ and $V(a,b)=(1-p)a+p(1-\beta)b$;
(ii) $\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)=V(1,0)=1-p$ and $V(\pi^w)=p(1-\beta)$;
(iii) $R(\pi^w)=(1-p)-p(1-\beta)=1-2p+p\beta$, which exceeds the printed value $1-p\beta$ by $2p(\beta-1)>0$;
(iv) for fixed $p\in(0,1)$, $R(\pi^w)\to\infty$ as $\beta\to\infty$ ('This is also unbounded for unbounded $\beta$' is unchanged); for example $R(\pi^w)=m$ when $p=\frac12$ and $\beta=2m$.

Printed. PDF p. 25: 'used the resulting estimate to compute $\mu_t(x)=\sigma(\hat\beta_0+\hat\beta_1^\top x+\hat\beta_2t+\hat\beta_3^\top xt)$.'

Corrected statement. With $Y_i=1$ if the horse lived and $Y_i=-1$ if the horse died, and $P(Y(t)=1|X=x)=\sigma(\eta_t(x))$ where $\eta_t(x)=\hat\beta_0+\hat\beta_1^\top x+\hat\beta_2t+\hat\beta_3^\top xt$ and $\sigma(z)=\frac1{1+e^{-z}}$:
(i) $\mu_t(x)=E[Y(t)|X=x]=1\cdot\sigma(\eta_t(x))+(-1)\cdot(1-\sigma(\eta_t(x)))=2\sigma(\eta_t(x))-1$;
(ii) $\tau(x)=\mu_1(x)-\mu_0(x)=2\left(\sigma(\eta_1(x))-\sigma(\eta_0(x))\right)$;
(iii) since $0<\sigma(z)<1$ for every real $z$, $2\sigma(z)-1\neq\sigma(z)$ for every real $z$: the printed formula is never equal to the mean of the outcome as coded.

Printed. PDF p. 26: 'For each individual feature in Figures 1 and 3, let $X$ represent the individual feature, and let $U$ represent the set of all other features. We use the full regression result as $\mu_0(x,u)$. To calculate $\mu_0(x)$ where $x$ represents the individual feature, we take the empirical conditional mean over the dataset: $\mu_0(x)=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n\mu_0(X_i,U_i)\mathbb 1(X_i=x)}{\mathbb 1(X_i=x)}$. Then, $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}$ is calculated empirically over the dataset as $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n|\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i,U_i)|$.'

Corrected statement. For every value $x$ with $\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbb 1(X_i=x)>0$, $\mu_0(x)=\dfrac{\sum_{i=1}^n\mu_0(X_i,U_i)\mathbb 1(X_i=x)}{\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbb 1(X_i=x)}$, and $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n|\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i,U_i)|$ as printed. Formal content: for real numbers $m_i=\mu_0(X_i,U_i)$, $i=1,\ldots,n$, and $G_x=\{i:X_i=x\}\neq\emptyset$,
(a) $\mu_0(x)=\frac1{|G_x|}\sum_{i\in G_x}m_i$, so $\min_{i\in G_x}m_i\leq\mu_0(x)\leq\max_{i\in G_x}m_i$ and $\sum_{i\in G_x}(m_i-\mu_0(x))=0$ (the empirical form of $\mu_0(X_i)=E[\mu_0(X_i,U_i)|X_i]$);
(b) $\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\mu_0(X_i)=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^nm_i$;
(c) the printed quotient is not this mean: its denominator has a free index $i$, and read as the number $1$ for matching observations it returns the group sum. For two observations with $X_1=X_2=x$ and $m=(1,3)$ it gives $4$, while the empirical conditional mean is $2$.

Printed. PDF p. 13, Appendix A.1, item 2: 'Untreated patients in the treatment population. In the main paper, we have assumed that the principal only observed outcomes $Y_i$ for treated units with $T_i^\pi=1$. If the principal also observed a fraction of outcomes for untreated units, with $T_i^\pi=0$, then these may also be incorporated into the estimate for $\hat\mu_0(x)$. Sufficient conditions for identifying $\mu_0(x)$ from a dataset of the agent's untreated units are: (a) No information asymmetry: the agent's treatment policy $\pi$ only depends on $X_i$. Under this assumption, we have no confounding: $\{Y_i(0),Y_i(1)\}\perp\!\!\!\perp T_i^\pi|X_i$. (b) Positivity: $P(T_i^\pi=0|X_i=x)>0$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$, and $Y_i$ is observed for a nonzero proportion of untreated units for all $x$.'

Corrected statement. Let $R_i\in\{0,1\}$ indicate that the outcome $Y_i=Y_i(T_i^\pi)$ of unit $i$ is observed. Sufficient conditions for identifying $\mu_0(x)$ from the observed outcomes of the agent's untreated units are (a) and (b) as printed, with (b) read as $P(T_i^\pi=0\mid X_i=x)>0$ and $P(R_i=1\mid X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0)>0$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$, together with (c) for all $x\in\mathcal X$, $E[Y_i(0)\mid X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0,R_i=1]=E[Y_i(0)\mid X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0]$. Under (a)-(c) and SUTVA:
(i) $\mu_0(x)=E[Y_i\mid X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0,R_i=1]$ for all $x\in\mathcal X$;
(ii) without (c): writing $q=P(R_i=1\mid X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0)\in(0,1)$, $m_1=E[Y_i(0)\mid X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0,R_i=1]$ and $m_0=E[Y_i(0)\mid X_i=x,T_i^\pi=0,R_i=0]$, conditions (a)-(b) give $\mu_0(x)=qm_1+(1-q)m_0$, so $m_1=\mu_0(x)$ if and only if $m_1=m_0$;
(iii) the stronger condition $Y_i(0)\perp\!\!\!\perp R_i\mid X_i,T_i^\pi=0$ implies (c).

Printed. PDF p. 7: 'This upper bound is tight up to a linear constant. Proposition 5. $\forall\epsilon>0$, there exist distributions of $X_i,U_i,Y_i(0),Y_i(1)$ wherein $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})\geq\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}-\epsilon$.' PDF p. 22: 'Proposition 5. For all $\epsilon>0$, there exists a distribution of $X_i,U_i,Y_i(0),Y_i(1)$ wherein $R(\pi^{w_{TT}})\geq\gamma_{marg}-\epsilon$. Proof. We construct a family of distributions of $X_i,U_i,Y_i(0),Y_i(1)$ that achieves this regret bound. Let $U\in\{0,1\}$, with $P(U=1)=\frac12$. Suppose $X$ is entirely uncorrelated with $Y_i(0),Y_i(1)$, such that $E[Y(t)|X]=E[Y(t)]=\mu_t$. For $\alpha>0,\beta>0$, let $\mu_1(x,u)=\begin{cases}0&\text{if }u=1\\\beta&\text{if }u=0\end{cases}$, $\mu_0(x,u)=\begin{cases}-\alpha&\text{if }u=1\\\alpha&\text{if }u=0\end{cases}$, which also means that $\mu_0(x)=\mu_0=0$ for all $x$. Suppose $\Pi$ includes all functions $\pi:\mathcal X,\mathcal U\to[0,1]$. Then by Lemma 2, and assuming $\hat\mu_0(x)$ is unbiased, we have $E[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=n(E[\pi(X_i,U_i)(\mu_1(X_i,U_i)-\mu_0(X_i))]$. The resulting policy $\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}}$ that maximizes this is $\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}}(x,u)=\begin{cases}0&\text{if }u=1\\1&\text{if }u=0\end{cases}$.' PDF p. 23: 'Suppose $\beta<\alpha$. Then the optimal policy is $\pi^*(x,u)=\begin{cases}1&\text{if }u=1\\0&\text{if }u=0\end{cases}$. with utility $V(\pi^*)=\frac\alpha2$. The regret $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})$ is then $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})=\frac\alpha2-\frac{\beta-\alpha}2=\alpha-\frac\beta2$. Note that $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}=\alpha$. For any $\epsilon$, choosing $\beta=\epsilon$ gets $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})=\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}-\frac\epsilon2$, satisfying the bound in Proposition 5.'

Corrected statement. The reward display reads $E[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=nE[\pi(X_i,U_i)(\mu_1(X_i,U_i)-\mu_0(X_i))]$ (the printed display opens a parenthesis after $n$ that is never closed); the proposition and the rest of the proof stand, the proposition being read, as its proof reads it, as the existence of a best response with the stated regret. In detail, take the construction of the proof with $0<\beta<\alpha$: $U_i\in\{0,1\}$ with $P(U_i=1)=\frac12$, independent of $X_i$ (the validator takes $X_i$ constant); $\mu_1(x,u)$ and $\mu_0(x,u)$ as printed, so $\mu_0(x)=0$; $\Pi$ the set of all functions $\pi:\mathcal X\times\mathcal U\to[0,1]$; an estimator satisfying the condition of target P006-T8 (for example the nonrandom $\hat\mu_0\equiv0=\mu_0$). Write $p_u=E[\pi(X_i,u)]$ for $u\in\{0,1\}$. Then:
(i) $E[w_{\mathrm{TT}}(\mathbf X,\mathbf T^\pi,\mathbf Y)]=nE[\pi(X_i,U_i)(\mu_1(X_i,U_i)-\mu_0(X_i))]=\frac{n\beta}2p_0$;
(ii) the best responses are exactly the policies with $\pi(X_i,0)=1$ almost surely ($p_0=1$), with $\pi(\cdot,1)$ arbitrary; the printed $\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}}$ ($\pi(x,0)=1$, $\pi(x,1)=0$) is one of them;
(iii) $V(\pi)=\frac{(\beta-\alpha)p_0+\alpha p_1}2$, so $\max_{\pi\in\Pi}V(\pi)=\frac\alpha2$, attained by the printed $\pi^*$ ($p_0=0$, $p_1=1$);
(iv) a best response with $E[\pi(X_i,1)]=p_1$ has regret $\alpha-\frac\beta2-\frac{\alpha p_1}2$; the printed one has $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})=\alpha-\frac\beta2$;
(v) $E[|\mu_0(X_i)-\mu_0(X_i,U_i)|]=\alpha$, so Assumption 2 holds with $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}=\alpha$ and with no smaller constant;
(vi) hence for every $\alpha>0$ and $\epsilon>0$, taking $\beta=\min(\epsilon,\alpha/2)$, the printed best response has $R(\pi^{w_{\mathrm{TT}}})=\alpha-\frac\beta2\geq\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}-\epsilon$;
(vii) (a version with a unique best response) replace $\mu_1(x,1)=0$ by $\mu_1(x,1)=-\eta$ with $0<\beta<\alpha$ and $0<\eta<\alpha$; then every best response has $\pi(X_i,0)=1$ and $\pi(X_i,1)=0$ almost surely, the welfare-optimal policy is still $\pi^*$, $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}=\alpha$, and the regret of the best response is $\alpha-\frac{\beta+\eta}2$; with $\beta=\eta=\min(\epsilon,\alpha/2)$ this is at least $\gamma_{\mathrm{marg}}-\epsilon$.
